1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:01,850
Where will the nut end up?

2
00:00:01,850 --> 00:00:03,550
Starting underneath
everything.

3
00:00:04,153 --> 00:00:07,923
Shake a jar of mixed nuts,
or a box of cereal,

4
00:00:08,143 --> 00:00:11,528
and the biggest pieces
often rise to the top.

5
00:00:11,638 --> 00:00:14,573
Scientists call it
the Brazil nut effect.

6
00:00:16,653 --> 00:00:19,128
You'd expect heavy
things to sink.

7
00:00:19,283 --> 00:00:21,468
Yet it happens even
when the big pieces

8
00:00:21,468 --> 00:00:22,428
are heavier.

9
00:00:22,887 --> 00:00:24,512
Here is one reason.

10
00:00:24,637 --> 00:00:26,487
Each shake lifts
the grains,

11
00:00:26,607 --> 00:00:29,702
and small ones tumble
into the gaps under it.

12
00:00:29,702 --> 00:00:31,132
It can't fit down there,

13
00:00:31,132 --> 00:00:33,132
so it's propped up
more each shake.

14
00:00:35,353 --> 00:00:38,888
Scientists have found
several effects at work.

15
00:00:38,888 --> 00:00:40,148
Grains can flow

16
00:00:40,148 --> 00:00:43,248
in a loop, up the walls
and down the middle.

17
00:00:43,448 --> 00:00:46,533
And in some jars,
the big ones sink instead.

18
00:00:50,050 --> 00:00:52,115
Measure it:
in this simulation,

19
00:00:52,440 --> 00:00:53,840
the big disc climbs

20
00:00:53,840 --> 00:00:55,895
from the bottom
to near the top.

21
00:00:56,175 --> 00:00:58,505
It takes about 10 seconds
of shaking.

22
00:00:59,220 --> 00:01:01,645
Shaking doesn't
just mix things.

23
00:01:01,645 --> 00:01:03,305
It often sorts them.

24
00:01:03,420 --> 00:01:06,355
Unpopped popcorn
kernels sink in a bowl

25
00:01:06,355 --> 00:01:07,795
for a related reason.

26
00:01:08,587 --> 00:01:11,383
Next: a coffee drop
dries into a ring.
